  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Cause, means and purpose",
      "summary": "Some prepositions tell why, how or for what purpose.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Some prepositions tell why something happens, how it is done or what it is for."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "He stayed home **because of** the rain.",
                "She cut it **with** a knife.",
                "This is a gift **for** you."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Because of and due to give a cause. With and by give a means. For gives a purpose. Because of takes a noun phrase, while because takes a clause."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Preposition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>cause</td><td><strong>because of</strong></td><td><strong>because of the rain</strong></td></tr><tr><td>means</td><td><strong>with, by</strong></td><td><strong>by email, with a pen</strong></td></tr><tr><td>purpose</td><td><strong>for</strong></td><td><strong>a room for guests</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
